# Front Matter

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:mdex, "~> 0.14"},
  {:yaml_elixir, "~> 2.12"}
])
```

## Intro

Front matter is a block of metadata at the top of a Markdown file, between two delimiter lines. Formats such as the [Open Knowledge Format](https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/data-analytics/how-the-open-knowledge-format-can-improve-data-sharing) use it to describe the document, while the body stays plain Markdown. The metadata is often YAML between `---` lines, but it can be any format, such as TOML between `+++` lines.

MDEx recognizes the block when you set the `front_matter_delimiter` extension option and stores its raw text in an `MDEx.FrontMatter` node. MDEx doesn't decode that text, so use a library for its format to turn it into Elixir data. This example uses YAML and [yaml_elixir](https://hex.pm/packages/yaml_elixir). See [Other formats](#other-formats) for TOML.

## Example

```elixir
markdown = """
---
type: BigQuery Table
title: Orders
description: One row per completed customer order.
resource: https://console.cloud.google.com/bigquery?p=acme&d=sales&t=orders
tags: [sales, revenue]
timestamp: 2026-05-28T14:30:00Z
---

# Schema

| Column        | Type      | Description                              |
|---------------|-----------|------------------------------------------|
| `order_id`    | STRING    | Globally unique order identifier.        |
| `customer_id` | STRING    | FK to [customers](/tables/customers.md). |

# Joins

Joined with [customers](/tables/customers.md) on `customer_id`.
"""
```

## Parse the front matter

Set `front_matter_delimiter: "---"` and the front matter becomes the first node of the document. The front matter must start on the first line. If it starts later, `---` is parsed as a thematic break.

```elixir
document = MDEx.parse_document!(markdown, extension: [front_matter_delimiter: "---", table: true])

[%MDEx.FrontMatter{} = front_matter | _] = document.nodes
front_matter
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%MDEx.FrontMatter{
  literal: "---\ntype: BigQuery Table\ntitle: Orders\ndescription: One row per completed customer order.\nresource: https://console.cloud.google.com/bigquery?p=acme&d=sales&t=orders\ntags: [sales, revenue]\ntimestamp: 2026-05-28T14:30:00Z\n---\n\n",
  sourcepos: %MDEx.Sourcepos{start: {1, 1}, end: {8, 3}}
}
```

## Decode the YAML

The `literal` field contains the delimiters. Remove them before decoding the YAML: the YAML parser reads `---` as the start of a new document, and `YamlElixir.read_from_string/1` then returns an empty map.

```elixir
{:ok, metadata} =
  front_matter.literal
  |> String.trim()
  |> String.trim_leading("---")
  |> String.trim_trailing("---")
  |> YamlElixir.read_from_string()

metadata
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  "description" => "One row per completed customer order.",
  "resource" => "https://console.cloud.google.com/bigquery?p=acme&d=sales&t=orders",
  "tags" => ["sales", "revenue"],
  "timestamp" => "2026-05-28T14:30:00Z",
  "title" => "Orders",
  "type" => "BigQuery Table"
}
```

Keys stay strings and the timestamp stays a string. To work with typed data, map the keys you expect into a struct. Don't call `String.to_atom/1` on keys from files you don't control: atoms are never garbage collected.

```elixir
defmodule Table do
  defstruct [:type, :title, :description, :resource, :timestamp, tags: []]

  def new(metadata) do
    {:ok, timestamp, 0} = DateTime.from_iso8601(metadata["timestamp"])

    %__MODULE__{
      type: metadata["type"],
      title: metadata["title"],
      description: metadata["description"],
      resource: metadata["resource"],
      tags: metadata["tags"] || [],
      timestamp: timestamp
    }
  end
end

Table.new(metadata)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%Table{
  type: "BigQuery Table",
  title: "Orders",
  description: "One row per completed customer order.",
  resource: "https://console.cloud.google.com/bigquery?p=acme&d=sales&t=orders",
  timestamp: ~U[2026-05-28 14:30:00Z],
  tags: ["sales", "revenue"]
}
```

## Render the body

The HTML output leaves the front matter out, so the same document gives you both the metadata and the HTML:

```elixir
MDEx.to_html!(document)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"<h1>Schema</h1>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Column</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>order_id</code></td>\n<td>STRING</td>\n<td>Globally unique order identifier.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>customer_id</code></td>\n<td>STRING</td>\n<td>FK to <a href=\"/tables/customers.md\">customers</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1>Joins</h1>\n<p>Joined with <a href=\"/tables/customers.md\">customers</a> on <code>customer_id</code>.</p>"
```

Without `front_matter_delimiter`, MDEx renders the block as content, a thematic break followed by a heading:

```elixir
markdown
|> MDEx.to_html!()
|> String.slice(0, 50)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"<hr />\n<h2>type: BigQuery Table\ntitle: Orders\ndesc"
```

## Putting it together

This module returns the metadata and the HTML of a Markdown file. A file without front matter gets an empty map, and invalid YAML returns an error:

```elixir
defmodule FrontMatter do
  @delimiter "---"
  @options [extension: [front_matter_delimiter: @delimiter, table: true]]

  def parse(markdown) do
    document = MDEx.parse_document!(markdown, @options)

    with {:ok, metadata} <- metadata(document) do
      {:ok, metadata, MDEx.to_html!(document)}
    end
  end

  defp metadata(%MDEx.Document{nodes: [%MDEx.FrontMatter{literal: literal} | _]}) do
    literal
    |> String.trim()
    |> String.trim_leading(@delimiter)
    |> String.trim_trailing(@delimiter)
    |> YamlElixir.read_from_string()
  end

  defp metadata(_document), do: {:ok, %{}}
end

{:ok, metadata, _html} = FrontMatter.parse(markdown)
metadata["tags"]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
["sales", "revenue"]
```

```elixir
FrontMatter.parse("# No front matter")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:ok, %{}, "<h1>No front matter</h1>"}
```

```elixir
FrontMatter.parse("""
---
tags: [sales
---
# Orders
""")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error,
 %YamlElixir.ParsingError{
   line: 2,
   column: 13,
   type: :unfinished_flow_collection,
   message: "Unfinished flow collection"
 }}
```

## Other formats

The steps are the same for any format: set the delimiter, remove it from the literal, and decode the rest. The delimiter can be any string. Hugo uses `+++` around TOML front matter, for example. In the `FrontMatter` module above, set `@delimiter "+++"` and replace `YamlElixir.read_from_string/1` with a TOML decoder such as `Toml.decode/1` from [toml](https://hex.pm/packages/toml).
